---
<Article
  eyebrow="Homelab · Build log"
  title="TradWeather, round two: mountain stations and a wind map"
  description="Three days that added a tab for the mountains, seven stations I do not own, a map with moving wind, and a layout that behaves like an app."
  date="October 2026"
  readingTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>The <a href="/homelab/weather-apps-suck/">first version</a> of this page
  answered one question: what is the weather doing on my hill. Then ski season
  got closer, and the question changed. The
  Green Mountains are close enough that one storm can put a foot on the summits and
  rain in my yard, and I wanted both on the same screen.</p>

  <p>Three days in October added a fourth tab for the mountains, seven live
  stations that are not mine, a real map with a moving wind layer, and a new layout.
  Most of it started from Porter Haney's
  <a href="https://www.porterhaney.com/upsouthskiforecast/">Up South Ski Forecast</a>.
  Porter's dashboard was also the starting point for the first version, so this is
  the second time I have rebuilt something of his for my own hill.</p>

  <p>The public code in <a href="https://github.com/tfarrell145/homelab-builds/tree/main/tradweather">homelab-builds</a>
  is still the first version. The Mountain tab is built around these particular
  mountains and stations, and it needs generalizing before it can join the public
  code.</p>

  <p>One note on the pictures. They come from a staging copy where my station is
  renamed "Home" and kept off the maps. Everything else is the live page.</p>

  <h2>A tab for the mountain</h2>

  <p>The Mountain tab has three parts: Snow, Conditions and Storm Types. Snow leads with a sentence ("Not ski season yet. Heavy rain coming Mon
  7 pm."), then tiles for the summit temperature, the freezing level, the next three
  days, nights cold enough for snowmaking, the odds of 1, 6 and 12 inches, and ridge
  gusts.</p>

  <p>The snow numbers come from Porter's storm recipe, re-run on
  <a href="https://open-meteo.com">Open-Meteo</a> at each spot's own height:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>How much falls.</strong> The average of five models (ECMWF, GFS with
    HRRR, ICON, the Canadian, and NBM). In upslope hours, when the wind about 5,000
    feet up blows from the west or northwest at 20 mph or more, the recipe trusts
    GFS alone.</li>
    <li><strong>What falls.</strong> Rain or snow comes from ECMWF's surface
    temperature, uncorrected. Sleet and freezing rain come from a warm layer aloft,
    checked only at the levels above each spot.</li>
    <li><strong>How fluffy.</strong> Ten inches of snow per inch of water, raised
    as high as 18 when the cloud sits between −12 and −18 °C, where the big
    star-shaped flakes grow.</li>
    <li><strong>What kind of storm.</strong> The wind direction aloft plus where the
    surface low sits, found as the lowest ECMWF pressure on a five-by-five grid.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>To check it, I replayed the January 25 to 26 storm from Open-Meteo's archive.
  My version gave Mt Ellen 11.8 inches; Porter had forecast 12. The phases came out
  in his order too: warm air first, then a coastal low (mine placed it about 480 km
  south-southeast, his 530), then upslope behind it. That replay now doubles as the
  sample on Storm Types, so the tab has something to show in October.</p>

  <p>Conditions is a cross-section of the range from the Champlain Valley over Mt
  Ellen to the Mad River side. Solid dots are live sensors. Hollow dots are the
  five-model average for that hour, adjusted for height at about 3.5 °F per 1,000
  feet, which means they cannot see a valley inversion. Below it are the two
  resorts' own reports, the National Weather Service's mountain forecast for Mt
  Ellen, and the webcams.</p>

  <p>Two things are still open. Porter backtests his temperature blend and mine is
  uncorrected. The summits have no public sensor, so up there it is always a model
  estimate.</p>

