  <h2>Seven stations that are not mine</h2>

  <p>A cross-section needs real readings along it, and every source had to be free
  and keyless. Most come from Ambient Weather's public map, which returns every
  public station in a box in one call. It is undocumented (it is the same request
  their own map page makes), so I cache it for two minutes and expect it to break
  one day. The App Gap reading is a Vermont road-weather station that Iowa State's
  <a href="https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu">Environmental Mesonet</a> republishes
  30 to 40 minutes late, so it counts as stale after 75 minutes and the rest after
  30. Synoptic, the obvious aggregator, is free only for universities.</p>

  <p>Picking a station uses the phone's own scrolling wheel: an invisible native
  select sits over the station name. Every station's Current view is baked into the
  page and CSS shows the chosen one, so switching costs nothing. The page grew from
  229 KB to about 465 KB to pay for that.</p>

  <h2>A map you can actually use</h2>

  <p>Radar stopped being its own tab and moved onto Current. Inline, the map ignores
  touch, because a live map in a scrolling page traps the scroll. Tap it and it goes
  full screen, and it pushes a browser history entry so the phone's back gesture
  closes it.</p>

  <p>Full screen copies Apple Weather's controls: a close button, one layers list
  (radar, wind flow, temperature, humidity, dew point, rain today, pressure), a
  locate button, and a card along the bottom for the radar scrubber or the legend.
  The station layers sit on USGS topo maps, which are public domain, with ski trails
  from <a href="https://www.opensnowmap.org">OpenSnowMap</a> once you zoom in.</p>

  <p>The basemap changed by force. CARTO started requiring an API key and drew "API
  KEY REQUIRED" across every tile. Radar now sits on Esri's keyless gray canvas.</p>

  <p>Wind flow is the newest layer. Every three hours the
  renderer writes a small grid of Open-Meteo wind, 10 by 16 points over New York,
  Vermont and New Hampshire for the next 25 hours, and the page animates particles
  across it. The first version covered the map's buttons and popups. Leaflet's
  panes all share one stacking context, so a canvas outside the map sat on top of
  everything, and moving it into its own pane fixed that. Speed is time-based, and
  I tuned it on the phone: 2.5 pixels per second per mph was too slow, 10 was too
  fast, 4 felt right. Tapping the map gives the model's wind for that spot, labeled
  "model, open terrain", because it runs about five times windier than a gauge
  sheltered by trees (Porter measured that too).</p>

