  <h2>What it cost</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>iOS 26 Safari reports a top safe-area inset of about 47 points</strong>
    even though the page already starts below the status bar, so every sticky header
    sat too low. The inset now applies only when the page runs as a home-screen app.
    Safari also tints its top bar from fixed elements and ignores sticky ones, so a
    3-pixel fixed strip stays at the top for that alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Test renders share the live API key.</strong> A burst of them got
    the live page rate-limited twice. The old page kept serving, which is the only
    reason nobody noticed. Test renders now go a minute apart.</li>
    <li><strong>Headless Chromium has blind spots.</strong> The version I use for
    screenshots has a 500-pixel minimum width, blanks screenshots of scrolled pages,
    and skips CSS transitions and map zoom animations. Measuring element positions
    worked better than trusting pictures.</li>
    <li><strong>A negative top margin collapses through its parent</strong>, so the
    parent's position already includes it. That cost an 18-pixel miss in the code
    that scrolls each sub-tab back to its top.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Credit</h2>

  <p>The storm recipe, the storm guide and the idea of a mountain tab are Porter
  Haney's, from <a href="https://www.porterhaney.com/upsouthskiforecast/">Up South
  Ski Forecast</a>. Station readings come from Ambient Weather's public stations
  and VTrans through the Iowa Environmental Mesonet. Forecasts and wind come from
  Open-Meteo, radar from NOAA through the Iowa Environmental Mesonet, and the maps
  from Esri, USGS and OpenSnowMap.</p>
</Article>
